hyprbars: buttons act on release over their own window; a release always ends the bar's drag;
hover is decided by input, not by rendering

The bar that started a press or drag handles its release wherever the pointer is (a layer
surface or another window used to swallow it and leave the window glued to the pointer).
"close", "minimize" and "zoom" run in-process on the bar's window. hyprbars:buttons N shows
the first N buttons only.

Hover used to change only inside the render pass, read before it changed, with mid-frame damage
to get a second frame; a pointer that stopped after one motion could get no glyphs at all. Now
pointer motion, presses, window updates and draw() sample one state (realtime titlebar::Hover,
geometry from titlebar too, so press and hover agree) and redraw from the event loop. All glyphs
follow that one state. Glyph textures are kept per monitor scale.

Inactive windows look inactive (macOS): bar_color_inactive and col.text_inactive colour the bar
and the title of a window without focus (0 = unset, same as inactive_button_color); focus is
tracked whatever the colours say, so a change redraws the lights and the title.

